{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261107T014701Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "6e85f4370dc45c55fcb9e35b", "title": "Detecting signs of hypertension using wearable sensors", "url": "https://research.google/blog/detecting-signs-of-hypertension-using-wearable-sensors/", "source": "The latest research from Google", "published": "2026-11-05T16:01:08+00:00", "is_ai": 0.94, "worthiness": 0.9575, "worthiness_confidence": 0.87, "final_score": 0.9575, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 7178, "delivered": true, "summary": "Google Research developed AI-PPG-ACC-HTN, a smartwatch-based AI system that detects hypertension using photoplethysmography and accelerometer sensors without cuff calibration. Trained on 492,347 hours of unlabeled data via self-supervised contrastive learning, the system aggregates 15-second embeddings across multiple days to identify persistent hypertensive states. In clinical validation against 24-hour ambulatory blood pressure monitoring, it achieved 65.8% sensitivity and 90.0% specificity for initial screening, outperforming office-based screening. For confirmatory testing, it reached 78.4% sensitivity and 96.7% positive predictive value, competitive with multi-day home monitoring. This passive screening approach could help close the global hypertension awareness gap by identifying at-risk individuals for clinical follow-up.", "jev_ms": 483.3, "jev_input_tokens": 2123, "jev_cost": 8.9166e-05, "llm_ms": 2336.6, "llm_cost": 0.002729, "errors": []}
{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261107T014701Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "6ddc6be03aecbbe87028b993", "title": "Formally verifying agent-written full-stack web apps with PDD Kit", "url": "https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/research/blog/formally-verifying-agent-written-full-stack-web-apps-with-pdd-kit/", "source": "Microsoft Research", "published": "2026-11-05T16:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.52, "worthiness": 0.9275, "worthiness_confidence": 0.76, "final_score": 0.9275, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "feed_full_text", "text_chars": 40493, "delivered": true, "summary": "Microsoft Research introduced PDD Kit, a toolkit for Proof-Driven Development that formally verifies AI-generated code by proving it meets specifications for all inputs, not just tested cases. The system uses Verus to mathematically prove Rust code correctness, falls back to tests for unverifiable components like external libraries, and shifts code review from line-by-line inspection to validating intent specifications. On a Pet Store login system, PDD Kit proved password and token logic while testing trusted boundaries like bcrypt and SQLite, catching wiring bugs tests miss. For teams scaling AI code generation, this approach replaces exhaustive review with formal guarantees on critical paths—start with high-risk components like authentication or payments.", "jev_ms": 463.8, "jev_input_tokens": 10697, "jev_cost": 0.000449274, "llm_ms": 2605.5, "llm_cost": 0.010857, "errors": []}
