{"type": "item", "run_id": "20261107T014701Z-routine", "option": "routine", "item_id": "f0247d2be6d23fda27989bba", "title": "The components of agent memory", "url": "https://www.anthropic.com/research/components-of-agent-memory", "source": "Anthropic Engineering Blog", "published": "2026-11-06T00:00:00+00:00", "is_ai": 0.99, "worthiness": 0.8975, "worthiness_confidence": 0.67, "final_score": 0.8975, "bucket": "high", "full_text": true, "content_source": "article_fetch", "text_chars": 21282, "delivered": true, "summary": "Anthropic describes a memory architecture for AI agents inspired by human neuroscience, where agents capture experiences in logs and use \"dreaming\"—offline compute during idle time—to consolidate and organize memories. Testing across CL-Bench and Pokémon tasks shows stronger models like Claude Opus 5.5 naturally judge what's salient to remember, while dreaming enables memory correction, wiki-building for knowledge navigation, and performance gains up to 48% on document QA tasks. The key insight: give capable models freedom to decide what matters rather than prescriptive rules, and use background compute to maintain accurate, organized memory representations.", "jev_ms": 130.9, "jev_input_tokens": 5165, "jev_cost": 0.00021693, "llm_ms": 2361.8, "llm_cost": 0.0056, "errors": []}
